<p>China said its economy grew 5.0% in the first quarter from a year earlier, beating analyst expectations of about 4.8%-4.86% and improving from 4.5% growth in the previous quarter. From the previous quarter, gross domestic product rose 1.3%, matching forecasts and edging up from the prior quarter&#x27;s 1.2% gain. This was the first GDP release since the outbreak of the conflict cited in the coverage, and several reports said the result suggests China remains broadly on track toward its annual growth goal for now. The stronger-than-expected result was driven by infrastructure spending, exports and fiscal stimulus, even as China&#x27;s domestic economy stayed under pressure from falling housing prices and weak consumer spending. At the same time, the conflict in the Middle East has added fresh uncertainty for the world&#x27;s biggest energy importer. Sources pointed to the risk of an oil shock that could raise factory costs, slow trade and weigh on the outlook later in the year.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/u_2tSkHGrOgyKii1w30r24Dxs8v79R1KdyRqFkdXP0-JI9fEFJ0xYWLBAzt5EV7hYUCPJ8--0AsGHZFycXfAe4HQjBojWg1Dkf2arTAF2CYFPESF5RuAJWWcAVPIuMcJy9-k-gzy6tJm3oAIvIhU-V2Z3z9bsUqOSloF96Iq_Cw0-GIr1VY_Bi8RIPDieTw1G_Q7UL0V' alt='An import and export trade show in southern China this week.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Infrastructure push: The New York Times reported that Beijing has poured money into new rail lines and other projects, helping steady growth even as households feel poorer because of the housing downturn.</li><li>Export support: The Financial Times said exports helped offset weak domestic demand in the first quarter, giving policymakers some breathing room despite softer spending at home.</li><li>No near stimulus: Analysts cited by the South China Morning Post said the 5% result may let China avoid additional near-term stimulus because the economy still appears on course for the full-year target.</li><li>Energy exposure: Because China is the world&#x27;s biggest energy importer and has an export-driven economy, Michael West Media said it is especially exposed if the Iran war triggers a sustained rise in oil prices.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Chinese policymakers: Officials presented growth data that beat forecasts as they prepare for possible fallout from the Iran war, including its effects on trade, costs and energy supplies. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/chinas-economic-growth-tops-forecasts-war-risks-loom/'>Michael West Media</a>)</li><li>Analysts cited by SCMP: They said the first-quarter reading shows China remains on track to meet its annual growth target without needing immediate additional stimulus. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/economy/economic-indicators/article/3350237/china-records-5-gdp-growth-first-quarter-track-annual-target'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>New York Times analysis: Its reporting said heavy state-led infrastructure spending is carrying more of the growth burden while consumers stay cautious because falling home prices have reduced household wealth. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/15/business/china-economy-growth.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Financial Times analysis: Its coverage highlighted a split economy, with exports and fiscal support cushioning an otherwise weak domestic backdrop. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/f2b53afc-be95-4788-bb03-9227d4bc7379'>Financial Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/chinas-economic-growth-tops-forecasts-war-risks-loom/'>China’s economic growth tops forecasts, war risks loom</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/15/business/china-economy-growth.html'>China’s G.D.P. Stronger Than Expected, Led by Infrastructure Spending</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/economy/economic-indicators/article/3350237/china-records-5-gdp-growth-first-quarter-track-annual-target'>China beats expectations with 5% growth in first GDP release since Iran war</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/f2b53afc-be95-4788-bb03-9227d4bc7379'>China’s growth hits 5% despite Iran war</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/chinas-economy-grows-5-quarter-shrugging-off-initial-132086607'>China&#x27;s economy grows at 5% in first quarter, shrugging off initial impact of Iran war</a> - abcnews.com</li></ul>